----- Original Message -----
From: "Loucks Guy" <Guy.Loucks@det.nsw.edu.au>
To: <tru64-unix-managers@ornl.gov>
Cc: "'Nikola Milutinovic'" <Nikola.Milutinovic@ev.co.yu>
Sent: Tuesday, April 11, 2000 11:30 PM
Subject: TRU64 and optimizing Disk Performance


> Original Post:
> People,
>
> I am looking for peoples feedback to the following situation:
>
> We have a data base structure consisting of some 3280 directories
containing
> about 6560 files. The directory structure is about 65MB in size. The
average
> file size is quite small.
>
> The change rate is fairly slow, say a couple hundred transactions / hour
> (This is absolute TOP rate).
>
> This is a WEB interface to our DNS management system. We need to be able
to
> search and locate entries in this structure rapidly (lets say sub 10sec).
I
> am interested in peoples experience and options.


First of all, set "bufcache" in such a way that the size of buffer cache
used by
UFS is larger than 70MB. Default is 3, 3% of your physical memory.
This is to fit entire directory blocks and related indirect blocks fit into
memory.


Second, bump up "nchsize" and "nchsz".
Set "nchsize" to more than the number of files and directories. This is to
fit
all the pathnames to search to be fitted in namecache to avoid unnecessary
directory I/O. And set "nchsz" to at least "nchsize/8". THis is namecache
hash
table size. The bigger the shorter than namehash chain size -> to speed up
name lookup. The better figure is nchsz = nchize/4

And set "ncache_valid_time" to long enough for namecache entries are not
expired soon. The default is 20 minutes, I think. When a namecache entry
is expired, the entry has to be re-read from the directory. But if all the
directory
blocks are in the memory, then this should be much problem, though.

UBC in UFS won't matter much since directories in UFS resides in buffer
cache.

I would recommend "noatimes" mount option. This avoid unnecessary
disk inode access time update.

I would recommend using "delayed" mount option. The option is
to delay all "synchronous" UFS metadata I/O. All the metadata will be
flushed to
disk on 30 second "sync", except when you are using smoothsync.
This will be especially good for proxy servers, since they have many
temporary
files created/deleted at all times. Performance impact is order of
magnitude.
One thing to note that since all "synchronous" metadata is delayed, there is
no guarantee of file system consistency upon panic due to absence of
write ordering. In the worst case, you need to newfs, but I have never
encountered such instance yet.
I am not sure which Tru64UNIX you are using but it is in v50a.
It will be available in v4.x streams very soon.


MFS is also a good solution only if you have enough physical memory for
all metadata and data blocks. But above parameter tuning works as good
as MFS without sacrificing physical memory.


If I left out something let me know.

-------

Chang Song
UFS, Tru64 UNIX, Compaq
song@zk3.dec.com


One small observation on AdvFS performance.

We have AltaVista firewall with web proxy caching turned on. This creates a
lot
of relatively small files. In our case 1.6GB cache had 120,000 files. Disk
performance was tragic, to say the least. The disk LED was constantly lit
and
all operation on firewall console was sloooow.

Then I decided to reinstall the firewall and to use special parameters for
the
domain holding cache, -x -p. The man pages for mkfdmn have optimal values
for
certain expected numbers of files. I used the parameters for 200,000 files
(I
was going to increase the cache to 2GB - 170,000 files).

When everything was set up and we let users connect to the Internet, I
COULDN'T
BELIEVE MY EYES. Disk LED was (and still is) flashing once in 3-4 seconds
and
firewall console (Web driven administration with Netscape as the client) was
even confortable to work on! That was the firs time I've ever seen such a
drastic change in behaviour after tuning.

So, don't write off AdvFS just yet. It takes some "understanding and care"
to
make it work properly.

Nix.
